WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.080
The first guest I wanna bring on,

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.460
is a friend of mine that
we have done this before.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.850
But she's got a new book out,

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.138
that I wanted to talk to her about.

00:00:08.138 --> 00:00:11.405
Please welcome Mandee Johnson.
Mandee.

00:00:11.405 --> 00:00:12.270
Hi.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.570
And she's odd just like that.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.720
Thank you for being here.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.730
For people who don't know you,

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.800
you're a successful comedy
producer in Los Angeles.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.740
You host a really one of the
best Indie shows that there is,

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.540
or just show period.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.640
And you've recently put out a book

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.251
called Super Serious, an
oral history of Los Angeles

00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:35.440
independent standup comedy.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.400
Here's the book right here.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.400
And you sort of capture
this time in LA standup

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.140
in the Indie scene from your
show mixed with portraits.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.610
Did I get that right?

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.214
Yeah, you did great.
Sounds like a thing.

00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:53.020
You did great. You did great, yeah.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.959
Very upset I didn't book the cover.

00:00:54.959 --> 00:00:56.290
(laughs)

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.380
You know, the publishers
pick the cover, truly.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.213
Yeah.

00:01:01.213 --> 00:01:04.220
Sent me a handful and
that one just felt fitting

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.560
and it was very silly.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.750
Was it something that you,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.320
that you always wanted to
do is to put out a book

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.560
or was it just like, I have all this stuff

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.090
and I mean this has to go somewhere.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.910
I mean, I think every
photographer on some level

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:18.283
always wants to put out a
book, you know, eventually.

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:21.100
The portrait series
definitely didn't start out

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.340
to be a book.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.270
It didn't start out to
be as big as it is now.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.420
But as it kinda grew bigger and bigger

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.330
around like year six of the show,

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.630
people started asking me
if I was gonna do a book.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.570
And then it kinda took
another couple of years

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.700
to find a book agent and sell
it and do all the things.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.940
I definitely did not know
it was gonna be a book.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.460
Had I known been a book, I
probably would have chose

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.930
a different face.
(laughs)

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.420
Because you would tell me
what to do one of two things

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.750
before the show would start.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.400
You're like hey, take a silly one.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.360
And then we'll do a serious one

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.795
to go along with the
show that you're curious.

00:01:56.795 --> 00:01:57.628
(laughs)

00:01:57.628 --> 00:01:59.140
So, there's some great photos in here.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.346
There's some beloved people in here.

00:02:01.346 --> 00:02:04.660
Our own Conan O'Brien makes
an appearance in the book.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.680
He does, yeah.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.260
He's mysterious, very fun,
Nicole Byer, great friend.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.606
He has some very funny whimsical shots.

00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:14.030
Here look at this,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.887
even Anthony Jeselnik is
showing off his slapstick.

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:18.837
Hmm.

00:02:18.837 --> 00:02:22.500
Then we get to the sad white man page

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.660
that I find myself on.
(laughs)

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.963
My depression era photo.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.860
You know, you're being very serious.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.620
You're taking the serious
prompt seriously, Moses.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.350
And I didn't appreciate that.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.810
Took it very serious.
Yeah.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.000
My whole family just died
of cholera and famine.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.800
Even, even Matt Broner known goof.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.130
Yeah, you guys end up being serious.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:45.963
You're doing it correct?

00:02:45.963 --> 00:02:48.179
Yeah, I think you nailed it.

00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:49.200
I feel great.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.700
Even known curmudgeon, Neal Brennan

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.400
looks a little bit happier than me.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.770
Yeah, he like turned those
slightly, you guys did a nice,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:00.285
proper like head-on, you know, I like it.

00:03:00.285 --> 00:03:03.160
Took the note too well.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.810
It's really cool because what
I really liked about the book,

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:06.800
I mean, part of it is like,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.360
you're nostalgic just to
see your friends in comedy

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.980
because we're all locked down right now,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.760
but there's great interviews in here.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.590
A lot of comedians that people
would know are in this book.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.290
And it's really cool for us to have,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.927
because it's the closest thing
that we have to a yearbook.

00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:23.790
Yeah. It's really, it's very fun.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.120
I mean, I didn't think we
were gonna be in a pandemic,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.371
when we scheduled the time
the book was gonna come out.

00:03:30.371 --> 00:03:32.800
We were trying to time it
close to just the ten year

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.310
of the show, which didn't
happen because of the pandemic.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.070
Yeah.
But it's been nice.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.911
I think to have it during this time

00:03:39.911 --> 00:03:44.130
where we all can be together
and we did have a little bit

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:49.130
of pre-pandemic comedy, like
memorialize in history forever.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.380
So.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.015
Yeah, although sad way to put it, but,

00:03:54.015 --> 00:03:55.940
(laughs)

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.365
but yeah, I get it was really
nice to read through it,

00:03:59.365 --> 00:04:01.610
and to see friends in the book.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.780
And just kind of remind of us the sign

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.330
that we really love
and that it's still out

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.950
there eventually.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.870
So it did make it a little easier

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.710
that one day in quarantine.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.381
Oh yeah, these are all the people in here.

00:04:14.381 --> 00:04:15.214
(laughs)

00:04:15.214 --> 00:04:17.302
Was there any interview in the book

00:04:17.302 --> 00:04:20.753
that surprised you of like
someone that maybe do the show.

00:04:20.753 --> 00:04:24.030
They didn't think it was gonna do the show

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.820
or talk to you for the book?

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:28.700
No, I think pretty much
everyone did chat with me.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.419
I really loved carrying
Kelly Garrett's interview.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:35.450
I thought her interview was
really great because she,

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.700
I think her time kind of
in and out of the scene

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.080
when she took a break, when
she was for work stuff,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.540
you know, she has a different perspective

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.947
on the value of it.

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:47.950
I also think that kind of
having the range of veteran

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.730
comedians like Andy Kindler and Karen

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.350
who have been around since like the '80s

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.320
to younger comics, like you
were interviewed for the book.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.870
You know, who've been around
LA for less time than that.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:59.799
Kind of, it's a nice to
show like the expansiveness

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:04.040
of the scene and how it kind
of like ebbs and flows to fit,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.160
however many comedians need it
to fit and it kind of morphs

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.210
around what comedy needs.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.150
So it was an interesting
thing to learn about,

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.210
as I interviewed people.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.110
It's really cool.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.480
And I would highly recommend the book,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.486
not just because I'm in it.

00:05:15.486 --> 00:05:18.247
In that it does exactly
what you said and it mixes

00:05:18.247 --> 00:05:22.010
those two things where it's
seasoned comics with very new

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.950
people, it's the same thing
you did with your live shows,

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.849
where I could go up with people
that I grew up watching and,

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:31.767
you know, have to follow them.
(laughs)

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:33.320
The same thing with a book of people

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.040
that are you know, just new to comedy.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.780
But we're in a book now,
technically a comedian

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.511
Yes, the best representation
of the independent comedy

00:05:41.511 --> 00:05:45.227
is that as big cities
where those comedians live,

00:05:45.227 --> 00:05:47.240
everybody starts to work on material

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.530
and the best place to do it,

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.770
a lot of times is independent
shows because there's just

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:51.918
more of them than the clubs.

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:56.380
And those audiences are more
patient because they pay less

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.820
money and more Indie scene in the process

00:05:58.820 --> 00:05:59.687
a lot of times, so.

00:05:59.687 --> 00:06:00.752
That's great.

00:06:00.752 --> 00:06:03.520
I think you captured a really
great thing in the book.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:04.610
Yeah, yeah.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:05.840
Super Serious.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.360
An oral history of Los
Angeles, independent comedy.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.490
Mandee, thank you so much for being here

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:13.063
and for making us a yearbook officially.

00:06:13.063 --> 00:06:14.996
Thanks for having me, Moses.

00:06:14.996 --> 00:06:15.829
It's good to see you.

00:06:15.829 --> 00:06:17.927
Thanks Mandee. Bye.
Bye.

00:06:17.927 --> 00:06:20.673
That's Mandee Johnson grab the book.

